description and check out them today,

00:02:46.109 --> 00:02:51.219
Although the Seven Deadly sins is largely
a Christian concept we do have similar ideas

00:02:51.219 --> 00:02:57.030
from the Ancient Greeks and Romans along many
other religions from across the world.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:01.569
The Seven Deadly sins are commonly paired
with the Seven holy virtues because they are

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:05.480
examples of how we should and should not behave.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:11.480
Aristotle sees this as less of a concrete
right and wrong system and more of a spectrum

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.099
of behaviour.

00:03:13.099 --> 00:03:19.639
Veer too far off in one direction and we move
into Vice territory,

00:03:19.639 --> 00:03:26.069
stay in the middle of these extremes and you’ll
be showing positive qualities or virtues.

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:31.790
One example would be fear, if you’re scared
of everything then you’ll miss out on things

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.920
in life because you’re allowing the fear
to control you, a demonstration of too much

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:37.920
cowardice.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.790
The quality or virtue that conquers fear and
cowardice is courage.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:47.520
However if one shows too much courage we now
lean heavily over to the other side of the

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.099
spectrum which is recklessness.

00:03:50.099 --> 00:03:55.389
Too much courage may leave you in situations
that could have otherwise been easily avoided.

00:03:55.389 --> 00:04:00.620
Rather than the approach of this behaviour
can only be good or bad, this is much more

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:09.209
of a balancing act trying to maintain a healthy
mean rather than the idea of Sin and Virtue.

00:04:09.209 --> 00:04:16.269
Anyway moving onto the Christian or more accurately
Catholic concept of the seven deadly sins.

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:21.170
The most concise definition I’ve come across
is as immoral behaviour that would separate

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:25.590
one from God and in turn then lead to more
sins being committed.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:31.530
A Venial sin would weaken one’s relationship
with God but not completely sever this bond

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.090
as the deadly sins would.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:38.440
It was believed turning your back on God would
make one more likely to commit what are known

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.470
as mortal sins.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:45.330
If you then die without repenting for these
mortals' sins you would go straight to hell.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:52.520
So the Seven Deadly Sins are essentially the
gateway or temptation that leads to more sin.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:56.960
These ideas were first discussed in the catholic
church by Pope Gregory the 1st.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:00.720
He wrote about these seven sins in the 6th
century,

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:06.010
a very similar idea was discussed several
hundred years before by the monk Evagrius

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:09.290
who instead listed 8 evil thoughts.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:13.560
These thoughts were translated from Greek
to Latin by John Cassian.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:19.170
Pope Gregory then streamlined this number
to 7 sins in his text Moralia and thus we

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.790
have the seven deadly sins.

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:28.640
Gregories list was then used in the 13th century
by Thomas Aquinas who further expanded upon

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:32.190
on the ideas behind these sins.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:39.250
The first sin is Pride which is also commonly
referred to as the father of all sin.

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:45.400
Pride however in this case refers to ego or
the idea of Hubris which is prevalent in Greek

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:49.780
and Roman myth as one of the worst crimes
one could commit.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:55.250
Whenever someone believed they were greater
or equal to the gods they were swifty punished

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:57.810
and made an example of.

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:03.800
Once one succumbs to pride this symbolises
a lack of humility, the welfare of others

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:10.700
is ignored in place of their own urges and
desires, this selfishness only leads to contempt

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:12.590
and other sin.

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:17.420
The most classic example of pride we have
is the story of Lucifer, the most beautiful

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.750
of God's angels.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:24.520
Lucifer began to question everything about
the universe he had been told.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:30.500
Eventually he started to believe that he could
do a better job ruling heaven than God himself.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:36.360
This led to a great war in heaven that Lucifer
would ultimately lose, for his rebellion he

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:42.280
was cast from heaven and forever known as
the fallen angel who personified the sin of

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:43.940
Pride.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:51.500
The opposite of pride is the virtue of humility
or humbleness.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:57.390
The next sin is Gluttony, this is the act
of over indulgence to the point of waste.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:02.500
Although gluttony is most commonly associated
with the consumption of food it can also refer

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:07.280
to an over indulgent lifestyle not too dissimilar
from greed.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:13.500
There are several different types of gluttony,
Thomas Aquinas divided gluttony into separate

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:14.500
forms.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:19.030
Eating food that was too expensive, luxurious
or exotic.

00:07:19.030 --> 00:07:21.920
Eating food in excessive quantities,

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:25.660
Eating food that required eliberate preparation,

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:28.360
Eating food at an inappropriate time

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:32.150
and lastly eating food too eagerly.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:39.190
The virtue of gluttony is Temperance or moderation
exercising restraint and modesty are examples

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:43.500
of how not to succumb to Gluttony.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:49.450
Moving onto Greed or Avarice as it’s also
known, this sin also focuses on desire.

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:54.930
The uncontrollable desire for material, social
or political gain.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:58.390
From this stems all sorts of questionable
behaviour.

00:07:58.390 --> 00:08:02.750
hoarding riches and materials that could be
used to help those in need,

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:05.520
stealing in order to obtain what you desire

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:12.390
or lies, trickery and the manipulation of
others to achieve status or political power.

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:17.060
Early European theologists would go as far
to see that other than the Devil there is

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:23.360
no greater enemy to man than the desire for
money, this was commonly used when discussing

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:24.450
Usury.

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:29.530
which is the practice of making immoral or
unethical loans which exceed legal interest

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:30.530
rates,

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:33.450
often taking advantage of others misfortunes.

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:36.570
This is what we would refer to today as a
loan shark.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:43.439
Similar to pride, Greed is considered a gateway
into numerous other sins and the route of

00:08:43.439 --> 00:08:46.250
many evils.

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:51.850
Lust is the capital sin that relates to desire
of a sexual nature, this was seen as leading

00:08:51.850 --> 00:09:01.330
to fornication, adultery, sexual assault,
seduction and beastiality in some odd cases.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:08.499
Most religions will distinguish lust from
passion, lust was an immoral desire and passion

00:09:08.499 --> 00:09:13.529
was widely more accepted as it was considered
God given and the main driving force behind

00:09:13.529 --> 00:09:15.079
reproduction.

00:09:15.079 --> 00:09:21.310
Lust does also come in some other forms such
as the unbridled desire for money, power and

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:22.810
even food;

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:27.940
these are minor forms of lust that also relate
to greed and gluttony.

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:33.700
The virtue opposite of lust is chastity, the
act of refraining from sexual activity that

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:39.630
is considered immoral and in some cases all
sexual activity.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:45.959
Envy is a sin that ties closely together with
both greed and lust, it is a desire for what

00:09:45.959 --> 00:09:50.800
others have that leads to resentfulness and
sadness.

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:57.740
This can mean desiring someone's possessions,
achievements, physical and personality traits

00:09:57.740 --> 00:09:59.800
or social status.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:04.959
In some cases you may not desire them but
instead wish that someone else didn’t have

00:10:04.959 --> 00:10:06.139
them.

00:10:06.139 --> 00:10:13.230
Envy is also separated into two categories
malicious and benign envy,

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:18.920
Malicious envy is similar to jealousy, the
negative emotion towards people who you perceive

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:21.290
as better off than yourself,

00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:26.820
benign Envy is merely the recognition that
someone is better off or in a better position

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:28.670
than yourself.

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:34.000
This type of envy is still negative but it
can be used in a positive way such as the

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.649
motivation to emulate someone's success.

00:10:37.649 --> 00:10:44.459
Malicious envy only leads down the path of
hatred, isolation and sorrow.

00:10:44.459 --> 00:10:49.860
The only sin that weighs down the souls more
than envy is pride.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:55.360
The most famous example of envy at least in
the book of Genesis is Cain murdering his

00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:01.860
brother Abel because he was jealous God favoured
his brother’s sacrifice.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:12.529
The virtue of this sin is kindness which involves
compassion and the satisfaction of one's situation.

00:11:12.529 --> 00:11:18.269
Sloth is the sin where you start to see the
separation from God we mentioned earlier.

00:11:18.269 --> 00:11:22.749
This isn’t laziness in terms of one just
sitting around the couch all day.

00:11:22.749 --> 00:11:27.529
Well actually it is but it also means a spiritual
laziness.

00:11:27.529 --> 00:11:33.279
The latin for sloth translates to mean without
a care, not caring about anything in your

00:11:33.279 --> 00:11:36.470
current life and even the after life,

00:11:36.470 --> 00:11:41.559
whatever the church perceived as obligations
or duties would be ignored.

00:11:41.559 --> 00:11:46.830
Losing interest in things in your life such
as work, hobbies, friends and romance leads

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:48.839
to sadness and depression.

00:11:48.839 --> 00:11:54.899
All the sins so far have been something someone
you should not do, acts you shouldn’t commit

00:11:54.899 --> 00:11:56.550
but do anyway.

00:11:56.550 --> 00:12:02.339
Sloth is the sin of omission not doing something
that you should do.

00:12:02.339 --> 00:12:08.779
You may have heard the phrases the devil makes
work for idle hands or Evil exists when good

00:12:08.779 --> 00:12:15.119
people fail to act which are taken from a
much older sentiment towards sloth.

00:12:15.119 --> 00:12:19.529
The virtue of sloth is diligence and a persistent
work ethic.

00:12:19.529 --> 00:12:25.110
The lack of stimulation relating to one's
mind and body is still something many struggle

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:28.730
to overcome even today.

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:34.680
The only sin left to cover now is Wrath, the
feeling of rage and anger that one struggles

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:36.110
to control.

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:41.910
From wrath stems hatred and vengeance that
causes feuds which can last for decades and

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:44.089
even centuries.

00:12:44.089 --> 00:12:49.589
Anger was not considered a sin unless it was
directed towards an innocent person, only

00:12:49.589 --> 00:12:53.069
then does it become the sin of wrath.

00:12:53.069 --> 00:12:56.839
Anger is normally divided into three subsections,

00:12:56.839 --> 00:13:02.230
passive anger consists of defeatism, self
blame and Apathy.

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:07.170
This was seen as the path that leads to substance
abuse and addiction.

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:12.619
Aggressive anger consists of bullying, hurtfulness
and vengeance.

00:13:12.619 --> 00:13:17.420
Assertive anger involves calling out behaviour
you deem as wrong, blaming the individual

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:21.480
responsible for that behavior and then punishing
them.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:24.540
The Virtue of Wrath is patience.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:29.499
Allowing people time to change the way they
behave.

00:13:29.499 --> 00:13:32.649
Hopefully you've enjoyed this video on the
seven deadly sins.

00:13:32.649 --> 00:13:36.769
I'm sure the comments comment sections will
be full of anime references I don't understand

00:13:36.769 --> 00:13:38.139
and that's okay.

00:13:38.139 --> 00:13:40.910
Maybe it's something I'll cover in the fututre.

00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:45.599
As Always, I've been your host, Mythology
and fiction explained.

